// Plain words for this work, by passage key. See ./index.ts.

export const PLAIN_ASCLEPIUS: Record<string, string> = {
  "herm.ascl.1": "Hermes (Trismegistus) tells Asclepius that God has brought him here to hear a sermon more godly than any they have spoken or been inspired to before. Whoever sees this teaching with the mind's eye will be filled with every good thing, if the \"many\" good things are really the \"One\" that contains \"All\": All belongs to One, and One is All, so tightly that they cannot be separated. He asks Asclepius to step out and call in the listener. Asclepius asks that Ammon may come too, and Hermes agrees: he has already written much to Ammon, as to a dear son, on physics and ethics, though this treatise will bear Asclepius's name. But no one else is to be called, because it would be impious to spread such a sacred teaching to the crowd. When Ammon has come into the holy place, the four sit in reverent silence, hanging on Hermes' words, and Divine Love begins to speak.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- that the good things one is filled with are many, and yet are really the One that contains All\n- that the good is not to be found in the many but only in the One in which All is contained",
  "herm.ascl.2.1": "Hermes tells Asclepius that every human soul is deathless, though not all in the same way or at the same time. Asclepius asks whether all souls are then of one and the same kind. Hermes chides him for losing his clear thinking so quickly: he has already said that All is One and One is All, since everything existed in the Creator before it was made, and God is rightly called All because his members make up the All. Throughout this talk, Asclepius must keep in mind the One who is All, the maker of All.",
  "herm.ascl.2.2": "Hermes says that everything comes down from Heaven to Earth, Water and Air. Only Fire, which rises, gives life; what moves downward serves Fire. Whatever comes down from above begets, and whatever flows upward nourishes. Earth alone, resting on itself, receives everything and restores every kind of thing it takes in. So the whole, made up of Soul and World, is always in flux and shows countless distinct qualities, yet is bound together so that all seems One, and All comes from One.",
  "herm.ascl.3.1": "Hermes says the whole Cosmos is made of four elements, Fire, Water, Earth and Air; the Cosmos is one, its Soul is one, and God is one. He asks Asclepius to give him his whole attention and all his insight, because the reasoning about the divine can only be grasped by a mind tuned to it. That reasoning is like a torrent crashing down from above, so fast that it outruns the attention not only of the listeners but even of the teachers.",
  "herm.ascl.3.2": "Hermes says that Heaven, the god we can perceive, directs all bodies, while the Sun and Moon govern how bodies grow and shrink. But the one who rules Heaven, its Soul and everything in the Cosmos is God, the Maker of all. From all these, under the same God, a flood of every kind of thing streams through the Cosmos and the Soul and through all of Nature. God has prepared the Cosmos as a container for every kind of form, and through the four elements he has stretched it up to Heaven, all to delight his own eye.",
  "herm.ascl.4.1": "Hermes explains how everything that hangs from above is divided into species. Each genus (a whole class) produces its own species (parts of the class). So the genus of the gods produces the various gods, and in the same way the genus of daimons, of humans, of birds and of all the animals in the Cosmos each produce species like themselves. There is also another kind, not animal but not without feeling either, which thrives in good conditions and wilts in bad ones: all the plants on earth that live by their roots and shoots, spread across the whole world.",
  "herm.ascl.4.2": "Hermes says Heaven itself is full of God, and the genera he has listed reach up as far as the places of the deathless species. Since a species is part of a genus, as a man is part of mankind, it shares its class's nature. So every genus is deathless, but not every species. The genus of the gods is deathless both in itself and in its species. Other genera last for ever too, because although they die in their individual members they keep going by being born again; so their members are subject to death: a man is mortal, but mankind is immortal.",
  "herm.ascl.5.1": "Hermes says the species of every genus are mixed in with all the genera; some were made earlier, and some are made from those. The ones still being made, whether by gods, daimons or humans, closely resemble their own genera. For no body can be formed without God's will, no species can take shape without the help of daimons, and no animal can be taught and trained without the help of humans.",
  "herm.ascl.5.2": "Hermes says that daimons who rise above their own kind and happen to join with a species close to the gods are counted as like the gods. Daimons who stay within their own kind love human reason, busy themselves with people, and are called daimons proper. It is the same with humans, or even more so: mankind comes in many different kinds, and because of the mixing just described it necessarily combines with almost every other species and thing.",
  "herm.ascl.5.3": "Hermes says a person comes close to the gods if he joins himself to them through his mind with godlike devotion, and close to the daimons in so far as he is joined to them too. But people who are content with the ordinary level of their own class, and the rest of mankind, will become like whatever kind of being they have attached themselves to.",
  "herm.ascl.6.1": "Hermes tells Asclepius that for these reasons a human being is a great wonder, a creature worthy of worship and respect. A human passes into God's nature as if he were a god; he knows the daimons as if he shared their origin; and, trusting his divine side, he thinks little of his merely human part. How fortunate is this human mixture! Joined to the gods by the divine part he shares with them, a person looks down on the part of him that belongs to the Earth. He binds to himself with love the other things he knows he is related to, through the heavenly order in him, and so he turns his eyes to Heaven.",
  "herm.ascl.6.2": "Hermes says a human stands in the blessed middle place, loving those below him and loved by those above. He farms the Earth; his quick mind mixes with the elements; his deep thought plunges into the depths of the sea; he puts a value on everything. Heaven does not seem too high for him, because his wisdom measures it as if it were close. No darkness in the air blocks his mind, no thickness of earth stops his work, no depth of water dulls his sight. He stays the same, yet he is everything, and everywhere the same.",
  "herm.ascl.6.3": "Hermes says that among living things, animals have roots that reach down from above, while plants grow from one living root upwards into a mass of green branches. Some are fed with two kinds of food, others with one. The two kinds are food for the soul and food for the body: the soul is fed by the constant movement of the World, and the body grows from food drawn from the water and earth below. Spirit fills them all and, mixed with the rest, makes them live; sense is added, and in humans reason too, given to humans alone as a fifth part out of the aether. Of all living things, God raises only the human sense to an understanding of the divine Reason. Hermes says that, since he feels moved to speak about Sense, he will give a sermon on it a little later, because it is as holy and mighty as the divine Reason itself.",
  "herm.ascl.7.1": "Hermes says he will now finish what he began: he was speaking about union with the gods, which only humans knowingly enjoy, meaning those who have been so carried away that they share in the divine Sense of intelligence, the most divine of the senses, found in God and in human reason. Asclepius asks whether all people's senses are not the same. Hermes says no: not everyone has gained true reason. Many chase wildly after a fake version of it, never see the real thing and are fooled; this breeds evil in their minds and turns the best of creatures into something like a beast.",
  "herm.ascl.7.2": "Hermes says he will explain Sense and related things fully when he talks about Spirit. A human is the only creature that is double. One part is simple: what the Greeks call the \"essential\" man, and what we call the \"form of the divine likeness\". He is also fourfold: what the Greeks call \"hylic\" (material) and we call \"cosmic\", from which the body is made. In this body the divine part is clothed, and there the godhead of Mind alone, with its kin, the senses of Pure Mind, finds a home and rest, as if shut inside the walls of the body.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- that besides the simple divine part, the human has a second part that is fourfold (made of the four elements)\n- that the human as a whole is fourfold as well as twofold",
  "herm.ascl.7.3": "Asclepius asks why, then, a human was planted in the world instead of living in perfect happiness in the part of the universe where God is. Hermes says it is a good question, and prays that God will give them the power to explain the reason, since everything depends on God's will, especially what is said about the highest Whole, which is the subject of their talk. He tells Asclepius to listen.",
  "herm.ascl.8.1": "Hermes says that God, the Lord and Maker of all, made from himself a second god, the visible one that can be perceived by the senses. He calls this second god perceptible not because it has senses itself (whether it does is a question for another time) but because those who see can perceive it. When God had made this first creation, second only to himself, it seemed beautiful to him, full of every good thing, and he fell in love with it as part of his own divinity.",
  "herm.ascl.8.2": "Hermes says that because this second god was so great and so beautiful, God wanted someone else to be able to look upon what he had made from himself, and so he made the human being, to imitate his Reason and his Love. God's will is its own fulfilment: as soon as he wills something, it is done. Seeing that the \"essential\" human could not care for all things unless he was wrapped in a cosmic shell, God shut him inside a body, decreed that all humans should be like this, and made him a single, well-balanced mixture of both natures.",
  "herm.ascl.8.3": "Hermes says God therefore made the human of soul and body, an eternal and a mortal nature, so that this mixed creature could honour both origins: admire and worship the things of heaven, and tend and govern the things of earth. By mortal things he does not mean water and earth themselves, which are deathless elements that nature has placed under humans, but what humans do with them and in them: farming, pastures, buildings, harbours, sea voyages, communication and mutual help. These are the strongest links between people and the earthly part of the Cosmos, which is kept up by knowledge and the arts and sciences; without them God does not want the Cosmos to be complete. Whatever God decides necessarily follows, and what has once pleased God will not stop pleasing him, since he knew long before what would happen and what would please him.",
  "herm.ascl.9.1": "Hermes tells Asclepius he can see he is eager to hear how a human can love and worship Heaven and the things in it. Loving God and Heaven, with all that is in it, is one continuous act of worship. No other living being, god or animal, can do this; only humans. Heaven and its hosts take delight in human admiration, adoration, praise and worship.",
  "herm.ascl.9.2": "Hermes says the highest God had good reason to send the choir of the Muses down to humankind: so that the earthly world should not seem crude for lack of music, but so that God, who alone is everything and the Father of all, could be praised in human songs with music, and so that even on earth the sweetness of heavenly praise would not be missing.",
  "herm.ascl.9.3": "Hermes says a few people, very few, gifted with pure Mind, have been given the sacred task of contemplating Heaven. Others, whose inner reason has not yet pulled free of the bulk of the body because of their mixed nature, are given the study of the elements and everything below them.",
  "herm.ascl.9.4": "Hermes says a human is a living creature who is not weaker for being partly mortal; rather, having mortality as well seems to make him better suited to reach sure Reason. He could not have borne the demands of both sides unless he had been made from both natures, so that he could both tend earthly things and love Heaven.",
  "herm.ascl.10.1": "Hermes tells Asclepius he wants him to grasp this teaching not only with close attention but with the living power of his soul. Most people cannot believe it; what is perfect and true is understood by holier minds. So he will begin.",
  "herm.ascl.10.2": "Hermes says the Lord of eternity is the first God, the Cosmos is the second, and the human is the third. God made the Cosmos and everything in it and rules them all, together with the human, who governs this mixed whole. The human takes the whole of it on himself and cares for it out of love, so that he and the Cosmos adorn each other; that is why, because of this divine arrangement, the World is so aptly called \"Cosmos\" (order, adornment) in Greek.",
  "herm.ascl.10.3": "Hermes says the human knows himself and knows the World too. He remembers what suits his own parts and keeps in mind what he must use so that it serves him, giving the greatest praise and thanks to God and honouring God's image, knowing that he himself is the second image of God; for God has two images, the Cosmos and the human.",
  "herm.ascl.10.4": "Hermes says the human is a single structure, but in the part made of Soul, Sense, Spirit and Reason he is divine, and seems able to climb from the higher elements up into Heaven. In his cosmic part, made of fire, water and air, he stays mortal on the Earth, so that he does not abandon everything placed in his care. So humankind is partly deathless and partly subject to death while it is in a body.",
  "herm.ascl.11.1": "Hermes says this double being, the human, has one chief ability: devotion (piety), which goodness follows. This ability seems complete only when it is strengthened by the virtue of despising all desire for things that are foreign to us. Everything on Earth that we own through bodily desire is foreign to our kinship with the gods; we rightly call these things \"possessions\", because they are not born with us but come to be possessed by us later.",
  "herm.ascl.11.2": "Hermes says all such things are foreign to a human, even his body. So we can despise not only what we long for but also the body, from which the fault of longing comes. A person should be human as far as growing reason leads the soul, so that by contemplating the divine he looks down on and disregards the mortal part joined to him, which he needs only to help look after the lower world.",
  "herm.ascl.11.3": "Hermes says that to be complete in both parts, a human is built of elements of each part in sets of four. He has hands and feet, both pairs, and other limbs, with which he serves the lower, earthly world. To these are added four more: sense, soul, memory and foresight, with which he can get to know divine things and judge them. So a human carefully investigates the differences, qualities, effects and quantities of things; but because he is held back by the heavy weight of the imperfect body, he cannot properly see the true causes of the nature of things.",
  "herm.ascl.11.4": "Hermes asks what reward a human should receive, having been made like this and given this service by the highest God: keeping the world in good order and devoutly worshipping God, fittingly obeying God's good will in both. Since the Cosmos is God's work, whoever preserves it and adds to its beauty with love joins his own work to God's will, when with effort and care and with his own body he shapes the species that God has already made by his divine intention. What reward should he have, Hermes asks, if not the one with which their ancestors were blessed?",
  "herm.ascl.11.5": "Hermes tells his devoted listeners that they pray this will please God's love: that when they have served their time, cast off the world's restraints and freed themselves from the bonds of death, God will restore them pure and holy to the nature of their higher self, that is, to the divine.",
  "herm.ascl.12.1": "Asclepius says Hermes has spoken rightly: this is the prize for those who devoutly put their lives under God and live to help the world. Hermes says that those who have lived impiously are denied the return to Heaven; instead they are assigned to move into other bodies, low ones unworthy of a holy soul. So, as their talk will show, souls living on earth risk losing their hope of future immortality.",
  "herm.ascl.12.2": "Hermes says some people find all this unbelievable, others think it a fairy tale, and others perhaps find it laughable. In bodily life, the pleasure of owning things is sweet. That is why spite, jealous of the soul's hope of immortality, locks the soul up, as people say, and holds it down in its mortal part, so that it never comes to know its divine part.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- that spite envies the soul's hope of immortality (as Mead's bracket supplies)\n- that spite envies the soul's immortality itself",
  "herm.ascl.12.3": "Hermes tells Asclepius, as if foretelling the future, that no one after them will have the single love of wisdom that consists only in knowing God (gnosis), in constant contemplation and holy devotion, because most people muddle philosophy up with all kinds of complicated reasoning. Asclepius asks why most people make philosophy so hard to grasp and confuse it with such reasoning.",
  "herm.ascl.13.1": "Hermes answers that they do it by mixing philosophy, through clever explanations, with various hard sciences such as arithmetic, music and geometry. But pure philosophy, which depends only on devotion to God, should deal with the other arts only so far as to appreciate how numbers work out the appointed positions of the stars when they come round again and the course of their movements. It should also understand the size of the Earth, its qualities and quantities, the depths of the Water, the strength of Fire, and what all these are and do, and so worship and praise the Art and Mind of God.",
  "herm.ascl.13.2": "Hermes says that to know true music is simply to know the order of all things and whatever God's Reason has decreed. When the order of each thing is skilfully brought together into one key, it makes, so to speak, the sweetest and truest harmony with God's own song.",
  "herm.ascl.14.1": "Asclepius asks what the people after them will be like. Hermes says they will be led astray by the cleverness of sophists and turned away from true philosophy, the pure and holy love of wisdom. True philosophy, untainted by the soul's crude curiosity, is to worship God with a single mind and soul, to respect what he has made, and to thank his Will, the one thing wholly full of Good. He says that is enough on this subject.",
  "herm.ascl.14.2": "Hermes says they will now turn to Spirit and related things. In the beginning there were God and Matter, which in Greek is taken to be the Cosmos; Spirit was with the Cosmos, or in it, but not in the same way as in God, and the things from which the Cosmos comes into being did not yet exist in God. They did not exist precisely because they were not yet, but were still in the state from which they would be born. Not only things not yet born are called unbegotten, but also things that lack the power to beget, so that nothing can be born from them. Whatever has the power to beget is generative: birth can come from it, even if it is born from itself alone, since birth can easily come from what is self-born, as everything comes from them.",
  "herm.ascl.14.3": "Hermes says God, everlasting and eternal, neither can be born nor could have been born. He is, he was, and he always will be; this is God's nature, wholly from itself. Matter (the nature of the Cosmos) and Spirit do not seem to be born from any source, yet they have in themselves the power to come into being and to produce, the nature of fruitfulness. The true beginning lies in a nature that holds within itself both the power and the material to conceive and give birth; such a thing produces itself without being conceived by another.",
  "herm.ascl.15.1": "Hermes says that, as distinct from things that can only produce by joining with another nature, this Space of the Cosmos, with everything in it, seems not to have been born, since the Cosmos plainly contains all of Nature's power. By \"Space\" he means that in which everything is. None of these things could have existed if there had been no Space to hold them, because everything that has existed must be given a place. The qualities, quantities, positions and actions of things that are nowhere could not be told apart.",
  "herm.ascl.15.2": "Hermes says that the Cosmos, though not itself born, holds the births of everything, since it provides for all of them the most fruitful wombs in which to be conceived. So it is the sum of that quality of Matter which has the power to create, though it was not created itself. And since this quality of Matter is by nature simply productive, the same source produces bad things as well as good.",
  "herm.ascl.16.1": "Hermes tells Asclepius and Ammon that he has not said, as many do, that God could not cut evil out of the world and drive it away; such people need no answer. Still, for their sake he will go on and give a reason. People say God should have freed the World from evil completely, since evil is so much a part of the World that it seems like one of its limbs. The highest God foresaw this and made all the provision that reason allowed, when he chose to give human minds sense, knowledge and intelligence.",
  "herm.ascl.16.2": "Hermes says it is by these gifts alone, which raise us above the other animals, that we can avoid the traps and crimes of evil. Whoever turns away from them at first sight, before getting caught up in them, is protected by divine intelligence and godly good sense, because the foundation of true knowledge is made of the highest virtues.",
  "herm.ascl.16.3": "Hermes says everything in the Cosmos is governed and brought to life by Spirit, and Spirit serves the will of the highest God like an engine or machine. We should think of God only this far: as the one who can be grasped by mind alone, called the highest God, who rules and directs the perceptible god (the Cosmos), which contains all Space, all Substance and all Matter, everything that produces and begets, and everything that exists, however great.",
  "herm.ascl.17.1": "Hermes says Spirit moves or rules every species in the Cosmos, each according to the nature God has given it. Matter, or the Cosmos, holds everything: it is the field of motion and what gathers all things together. God rules it, giving every cosmic thing what it needs, and he fills all things with Spirit according to the nature of each.",
  "herm.ascl.17.2": "Hermes says that because the hollow Cosmos turns round in the shape of a sphere, its very form means it can never see the whole of itself. However high a point in it you chose to look down from, you could not see the bottom from there. Yet because it meets the senses in many places, people think it can be seen throughout. It only seems fully visible because of the forms of the species that appear carved on it like images; in fact it is always invisible to itself.",
  "herm.ascl.17.3": "Hermes says that the bottom or lowest part of the sphere, if a sphere can have such a place, is called a-eides in Greek, because eidein in Greek means \"to see\", and that region cannot be seen. That is also why the species are called eideai, since their form cannot be seen. Because they cannot be seen they are called Hades in Greek, and because they are at the bottom of the sphere they are called Inferi in Latin. These, then, come first and are the sources and heads of everything that is in them, through them or from them.",
  "herm.ascl.18.1": "Asclepius sums up: so these things, as Hermes says, are in themselves the cosmic principles of all the species in them, the sum and substance of each. Hermes replies that the Cosmos feeds bodies, Spirit feeds souls, and the higher Sense, a heavenly gift given only to humans, feeds the mind. And not all humans, only a few, have minds able to receive so great a blessing.",
  "herm.ascl.18.2": "Hermes says that just as the Sun lights up the World, that Light lights up the human mind, and even more fully. Whatever the Sun shines on is soon robbed of its light when the Earth or Moon gets in the way or night falls. But once the higher Sense has blended with a human soul, the happy union of their natures makes them one, so that such minds are never again held by the errors of darkness. That is why it is rightly said that the higher Senses are the souls of gods; Hermes adds, not of all gods, only of the great ones, and indeed of their first principles.",
  "herm.ascl.19.1": "Asclepius asks what Hermes means by the heads of things, or the sources of beginnings. Hermes says he is revealing great mysteries and divine secrets, so he begins with prayers for Heaven's favour. There are many ranks of gods; one class is called the Noumenal (grasped by the mind), the other the Sensible (perceived by the senses). The Noumenal gods are not so called because they are beyond our senses: we actually perceive them more truly than the ones we call visible, as the argument will show and as Asclepius will be able to see if he pays attention. For a lofty teaching, especially one too divine for ordinary minds, will fly past the listeners, or flow back and rejoin its own source, unless they listen very carefully.",
  "herm.ascl.19.2": "Hermes says there are certain gods who are the principles of all the species. Next come those whose essence is their principle: the Sensible gods, each like its double source, who act on everything through the senses, each part making the proper work of every single thing shine through the other. The essence-chief of Heaven, or whatever the word Heaven covers, is Zeus, because Zeus gives life to everything through Heaven. The Sun's essence-chief is light, because the gift of light is poured on us through the Sun's disk.",
  "herm.ascl.19.3": "Hermes says the \"Thirty-six\", called Horoscopes, are in the same region as the fixed stars; their essence-chief or prince is the one called Pantomorph or Omniform (the All-shaped), who makes the different forms for different species. The \"Seven\", called spheres, each have their own ruler; together these are called Fortune and Heimarmene (Fate), by which all things change according to nature's law, constant stability being varied by unceasing motion. The Air is the engine or machine through which everything is made, mortal from mortal things and the like; it too has an essence-chief, a second Air.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- that the Air is the instrument through which mortal things are made from mortal things\n- that the Air's own ruler, a second Air, is the one who makes mortal things from mortal things",
  "herm.ascl.19.4": "Hermes says these ranks of gods, ordered like this from bottom to top, are linked to one another and tend towards one another, so that mortal things are bound to mortal ones and perceptible things to perceptible ones. Yet to the highest Lord the whole of this great order of rule seems not to be many things, but one. Everything hangs from and flows down from the One; seen separately, things seem as many as can be, but united they are one, or rather two, from which everything is made: Matter, through which other things are made, and the Will of the One by whose nod they come about.",
  "herm.ascl.20.1": "Asclepius asks whether this is the reason again. Hermes says it is. God is the Father or Lord of all, or whatever holier name people give him, which they should keep among themselves for the sake of understanding. If we contemplate this utterly transcendent God, none of these names will define him. A spoken word is a sound made by breath striking the air, expressing a person's whole will or a higher sense grasped by the mind; a name is a syllable or two, shaped so that it can link voice and ear. In the same way, God's full Name would have to consist of Sense, Spirit and Air, and everything in them, through them or with them.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- that the holier names for God should be kept private among themselves\n- that such names are kept apart only for the sake of understanding, not because they define God",
  "herm.ascl.20.2": "Hermes says he does not expect that the Creator of all greatness, the Father and Lord of everything, could ever have a single name, even one made up of many parts. He cannot be named, or rather he can be called by every name. Because he is One and All, either all things must be called by his name, or he must be called by the names of all things.",
  "herm.ascl.20.3": "Hermes says God alone, yet complete in the fruitfulness of both sexes, always pregnant with his own Will, always gives birth to whatever he wants to bring forth. His Will is complete goodness, which is also the goodness of all things, born from his own divine nature, so that all things may exist as they always have, and so that from now on the power to be born from themselves will be enough for everything that is to be born. That, he tells Asclepius, is the reason why and how everything is made of both sexes.",
  "herm.ascl.21.1": "Asclepius asks whether Hermes is speaking of God. Hermes says: not only of God, but of everything, living and lifeless, since nothing that exists can be barren. If fruitfulness were taken away from things, they could not go on being what they are for ever. Nature, Sense and the Cosmos all have in themselves the power to bring things to birth and to preserve what is born. Each sex is full of the power to procreate, and between them there is a union, or more truly an incomprehensible unity, which can rightly be called Eros or Aphrodite, or both.",
  "herm.ascl.21.2": "Hermes says it is truer than all truth and clearer than anything the mind sees that from the universal God of universal Nature every other thing has received, for ever, the mystery of bringing forth; and in it are born the sweetest affection, joy, merriment, longing and divine love. They might have needed to describe the great power and pull of this mystery, if everyone could not know it from their own experience by observing themselves.",
  "herm.ascl.21.3": "Hermes describes the climax of sexual union, when one partner passes the seed to the other, who eagerly receives it and draws it deep within (Mead's text marks a short break here). At that moment, out of their shared union, the female takes on the nature of the male, and the male grows tired with female languor. That is why this sweet and necessary mystery is carried out in private, so that the divine power of either sex should not be made to blush at natural union because of the crude jokes of the ignorant, still less if it were exposed to the eyes of impious people.",
  "herm.ascl.22.1": "Hermes says devout people are few, so few they could be counted, even in the whole world. So evil lives in the many, because they lack knowledge (gnosis) and discernment of the things that are. It is from understanding the divine Reason that orders everything that contempt for vice and a cure for it arise throughout the world. But where ignorance persists, every vice grows strong and torments the soul with incurable wounds, so that, infected and corrupted, it swells up as if poisoned, except in those who know the discipline of souls and the highest cure of the mind.",
  "herm.ascl.22.2": "Hermes says that, even if it helps only a few, he should set out why the Divine has chosen to share knowledge and intelligence with humans alone, and asks them to listen. When God, Father and Lord, made the human after the gods, out of an equal mix of a less pure cosmic part and a divine part, the flaws of the cosmic part naturally stayed mixed into human bodies. Further flaws came from the food and sustenance we must share with all living things. Because of these, desires, passions and other vices of the mind were bound to occupy human souls.",
  "herm.ascl.22.3": "Hermes says the gods were made from the finest part of Nature and need no support from reason and discipline; their deathlessness, the strength of staying always the same age, serves them as wisdom and knowledge. Even so, for the sake of the unity of reason, God gave them by his everlasting law a fixed order bounded by the necessity of law, in place of knowledge and intellect, so that they would not be strangers to these. Humans, however, he set apart from all other animals by reason and discipline alone, through which they can remove and separate out the vices of their bodies, and he helps them in their hope and effort towards deathlessness.",
  "herm.ascl.22.4": "Hermes says that, in short, God made the human good and able to share in immortal life, out of two natures, one mortal and one divine. Because he was made like this by God's will, it is decreed that the human should be higher both than the gods, who are made only of immortal nature, and than all mortal things. That is why humans, related to the gods, honour them with devotion and a holy mind, while the gods, in turn, look with kindly sympathy on all human affairs and protect them.",
  "herm.ascl.23.1": "Hermes says this is true only of a few people with devout minds; nothing should be said of the wicked, so as not to spoil a sacred sermon by thinking of them. Since the talk is about the relationship between humans and gods, he tells Asclepius to learn the power and strength of human beings. Just as the Lord and Father, the highest God, created the gods in Heaven, so humans make the gods in the temples, who let people come near them; these gods not only receive light but give out light. A human not only advances towards the gods but also establishes gods on earth. He asks Asclepius whether he is surprised, or whether even he does not believe it.",
  "herm.ascl.23.2": "Asclepius says he is amazed, but gladly agrees with everything Hermes says, and thinks the person who has reached such happiness most blessed. Hermes agrees that such a person deserves wonder, since he is the greatest of all. As for the gods in Heaven, it is clear from how they are composed that they were conceived from the finest part of nature, and that the only signs by which they are recognised are, so to speak, chiefly their heads.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- that the heavenly gods are recognised mainly by their heads\n- that the heads are the chief, or only, part by which they can be pictured",
  "herm.ascl.23.3": "Hermes says the kind of gods that humans make is formed from both natures: from the older and far more divine nature, and from the human one, that is, from the material humans are made of and shaped from, not only in their heads but in every limb and their whole body. So, in picturing the Divine, humankind remembers its own nature and origin. Just as the Father and Lord made the eternal gods to be like himself, so humankind has shaped its own gods in the likeness of its own appearance.",
  "herm.ascl.24.1": "Asclepius asks whether Hermes means their statues. Hermes says yes, statues, and notes that even Asclepius doubts it: statues given soul and sense and filled with spirit, which do great and strange things; statues that can foresee the future and perhaps prophesy, foretelling things in dreams and many other ways; statues that weaken people or cure their grief, as they deserve. He asks whether Asclepius does not know that Egypt is the image of Heaven, or rather the place where everything governed and done in Heaven has been transferred and come down; more truly still, their land is the shrine of the whole World.",
  "herm.ascl.24.2": "Hermes says that since the wise should know things in advance, they must not be ignorant of this. A time will come when it will seem that Egypt has worshipped the Divine devoutly and faithfully for nothing, and all its holy worship will come to nothing. The Divine is about to hurry back from Earth to Heaven, and Egypt will be abandoned; the land that was the home of devout worship will be left bereaved, widowed of the gods. Foreigners will fill the country, and not only will the old worship be neglected, but, worse still, laws will be passed to punish it and ban the worship of the gods entirely.",
  "herm.ascl.24.3": "Hermes says this holiest land, home of shrines and temples, will then be choked with tombs and corpses. \"O Egypt, Egypt,\" he says, only stories of your worship will be left, as unbelievable to your own children as to others, and only words carved on your stones will tell of your devout deeds. Egypt will become home to the Scythian or the Indian or some such foreign neighbour. The gods will go back up to Heaven, their abandoned worshippers will all die out, and Egypt, left without gods or people, will be deserted.",
  "herm.ascl.24.4": "Hermes addresses the river, the holiest stream, and tells it what will happen: it will burst its banks with torrents of blood; its divine waters will not only be stained with blood but will overflow with it. The dead will far outnumber the living, and those who survive will be Egyptian only in their language, but foreigners in how they act.",
  "herm.ascl.25.1": "Hermes asks Asclepius why he is weeping: far worse is coming, and Egypt herself will be driven to greater evils and stained by them. The holy land, once rightly most loved by God because of her devout service to the gods on earth, the only colony of holiness and the world's teacher of religion, will become the model of everything most barbarous. Then, out of disgust with humankind, the World will no longer seem worth admiring and praising. This whole good thing, the most beautiful sight there has ever been, is, or will be, will be in danger.",
  "herm.ascl.25.2": "Hermes says the World will become a burden to people, so they will despise it and stop loving the Cosmos as a whole: God's unchanging work, the glorious structure of the Good made of countless forms, the instrument of God's Will that freely supports his work, the vast whole gathered into one, which ought to be honoured, praised and loved, at least by those with eyes to see. Darkness will be preferred to Light and Death to Life. No one will look up to Heaven; the devout will be thought mad and the impious wise, the frenzied strong and the worst the best.",
  "herm.ascl.25.3": "Hermes says the soul and everything about it, including its belief that it was born deathless or will reach deathlessness, as he has taught them, will be treated as a joke and as empty nonsense. Believe him, he says: the death penalty will be decreed for anyone who devotes himself to the religion of the mind. New laws will come in, and nothing sacred or devout, nothing worthy of Heaven or the gods in Heaven, will be heard or even believed in the heart.",
  "herm.ascl.25.4": "Hermes says the gods will sorrowfully leave humankind; only evil angels will remain. Mixing with people, they will seize them and drive the wretched to every kind of reckless wrong: wars, robbery, deceit and everything contrary to the soul's nature. Then the Earth will no longer hold firm, the sea will no longer be sailed, and the heavens will no longer keep the courses of the stars. Every god's voice will fall into a great silence that no one can break; the fruits of the earth will rot, the Earth will stop bearing, and the Air itself will grow faint and listless.",
  "herm.ascl.26.1": "Hermes says that when this comes it will be the World's old age: impiety, disorder and the loss of reason in all good things. When all this has happened, God, the Lord and Father, first in power and ruler of the one visible god, will check wrongdoing and call error back from corruption to good ways and to deeds in keeping with his Will, that is, God's Goodness. He will end all evil by washing it away with a flood, burning it away with fire, or sweeping it away with plagues spread through hostile lands, and will bring the Cosmos back to its original form. Then the World will again seem worth worshipping and admiring, and the people of that time will praise and bless God, maker and restorer of so great a work, without ceasing.",
  "herm.ascl.26.2": "Hermes says this rebirth of the Cosmos is the renewal of all good things and the most holy and devout restoration of Nature itself, over a fixed course of time; Nature has no beginning and no end, because God's Will has no beginning and, staying always the same, has no end. Asclepius suggests that this is because God's nature is the deliberate decision of his Will, and asks whether that decision is the highest good.",
  "herm.ascl.26.3": "Hermes tells Asclepius that will is the child of that decision, and willing itself comes from the Will. God does not will anything because he wants it, since he is the fullness of all things and wills what he already has. So he wills every good thing and has everything he wills; or rather, he thinks and wills all good. That is God, and the World of Good is his image.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- that will is born from God's deliberate decision\n- that willing comes from the Will itself, and the decision comes after",
  "herm.ascl.27.1": "Asclepius asks whether the Cosmos is good. Hermes says it is, as he will explain. Just as God gives out and manages good things for all the kinds of being in the Cosmos, namely Sense, Soul and Life, so the Cosmos gives everything that seems good to mortals: the changing of its parts, fruits in their seasons, birth, growth, ripeness and the like. That is why God rises above the height of the highest Heaven, reaches everywhere, and sees all things on every side.",
  "herm.ascl.27.2": "Hermes says that beyond Heaven stretches a starless space, foreign to everything that has a body. The one who manages the region between Heaven and Earth, which we call Zeus Above, rules it. Earth and sea are ruled by Zeus Below, who feeds mortal lives and fruit trees. Through the powers of all these, fruit, trees and the earth grow green. The powers and energies of the other gods will be spread through everything that exists.",
  "herm.ascl.27.3": "Hermes says those who rule the earth will be spread through all the lands and finally gathered into a city at the top of upper Egypt, which will be founded towards the setting sun and to which the whole human race will hurry. Asclepius asks where they are right now. Hermes says they are gathered in a very large community on the Libyan hill, and that enough has been said about this.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- that the earth's rulers will be gathered into a city on the edge of upper Egypt, to the west\n- that the city will be founded in the west, beyond the top of Egypt",
  "herm.ascl.27.4": "Hermes says they must now discuss deathlessness and death. Ordinary people, who do not know true Reason, are tortured by hoping about and fearing death. Death happens when the body, worn out by toil, comes apart, and when the number that holds the body's parts together as one machine for living is complete. The body dies when it can no longer support a person's life-powers. So death is the dissolving of the body and the end of bodily sensation. There is no need to worry about this death. But there is another one that no one can escape, which ignorant and unbelieving people think little of.",
  "herm.ascl.27.5": "Asclepius asks what it is that people know nothing about or do not believe can happen. Hermes tells him to listen.",
  "herm.ascl.28.1": "Hermes says that when the soul leaves the body, judging and weighing its worth passes into the power of its highest daimon (guardian spirit). If he finds it devout and just, he lets it rest in places suited to it. But if he finds it stained with evil deeds and fouled with vice, he throws it down from above into the depths and hands it over to warring storms and whirlwinds of air, fire and water.",
  "herm.ascl.28.2": "Hermes says such a soul is dragged back and forth between Heaven and Earth on the waves of the Cosmos, racked with endless pain, so that its deathless nature becomes its torment: because it never stops feeling, it carries the yoke of endless torture. So we should dread this, be afraid, and always be on guard not to be caught up in it. Those who do not believe now will be made to believe after their wrongdoing, by facts and not words, by actually suffering punishment and not by threats.",
  "herm.ascl.28.3": "Asclepius asks whether people's wrongs are not punished by human law alone. Hermes says, first, that everything on Earth must die. And everything that lives through a body and stops living through it receives fitting reward or punishment according to the merits or faults of its life. The punishments are all the harsher if the wrongs stayed hidden during life until death, because then the divine power makes them fully aware of everything as it really is, according to the degree of punishment assigned to their crimes.",
  "herm.ascl.29.1": "Asclepius asks whether these deserve even greater punishment. Hermes says they certainly do: people condemned by human laws lose their lives by violence, so that everyone can see they did not give up their souls by natural death but paid the penalty they deserved. The righteous person, on the other hand, is defended by serving God and by deep devotion, for God guards such people from every evil.",
  "herm.ascl.29.2": "Hermes says the Father of all, the Lord, who alone is everything, loves to show himself to everyone. He does not shine his light on a person because of where he is, what qualities he has, or how great he is, but through the mind's understanding alone: on the one who shakes the clouds of error from his soul, catches sight of the brilliance of Truth, and blends himself with the all-perceiving sense of the divine Intelligence. Through love of it he is freed from the part of himself that Death rules, and the seed of confidence in his future deathlessness is planted in him.",
  "herm.ascl.29.3": "Hermes says this is how the good will differ from the bad. Each good person will shine in devotion, holiness, good sense, worship and service of God, and will see true Reason as if with bodily eyes; and by the confidence of his belief he will outshine other people as the Sun outshines the stars. For the Sun lights the other stars not so much by the greatness of his light as by his holiness and divinity. Asclepius should regard the Sun as the second god, ruling everything and giving light to every living thing in the Cosmos, with soul or without. For if the Cosmos is a living being that has always lived, lives now and always will, nothing in it is subject to death: every part of an ever-living thing is as ever-living as the whole, and in the World, which is for everyone and also an ever-living thing, there is no room for death. (In Mead's printing this chapter has no paragraph 4.)",
  "herm.ascl.29.5": "Hermes says the Sun should therefore be the full store of life and deathlessness, if he must live for ever. So the Sun, like the Cosmos, lasts for ever, and for ever rules all the life-powers, or gives them out. God, then, is the eternal ruler of all living things and living functions in the World, the everlasting giver of Life itself. He gave life once and for all to every life-power, and he keeps them going by a law that lasts for ever, as Hermes will now explain.",
  "herm.ascl.30.1": "Hermes says the Cosmos moves within the very life of Eternity, and the space of the Cosmos lies within the everlastingness of Life. So it will never stop or be destroyed, because it is fenced in and bound together by the everlastingness of living. The Cosmos gives life to everything in it and is the space of everything governed beneath the Sun. Its motion has a double energy: it is given life from outside by Eternity, and it gives life to everything inside it, making them all different according to fixed numbers and times.",
  "herm.ascl.30.2": "Hermes says that on Earth time is marked by the state of the air, by changes of heat and cold; in Heaven it is marked by the stars returning to the same places as their course comes round. The Cosmos is the home of Time and is kept fresh by Time's course and movement, while Time is kept by regular order. Order and Time renew everything in the Cosmos by taking turns.",
  "herm.ascl.30.3": "Hermes says that, this being so, nothing that is born, in Heaven or on Earth, is stable, fixed or unmoving. Only God is unmoving, and rightly so, because he is in himself, by himself and around himself, completely full and perfect. He is his own unmoving stability; no one else's pressure can move him, nor is he in anyone else's space.",
  "herm.ascl.30.4": "Hermes says all spaces are in God, and he alone is in all of them, unless someone dares to say that God's own motion is in Eternity; but rather God is unmoving Eternity itself, into which the motion of all times flows back and from which it begins.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- that one might say God's motion takes place within Eternity\n- that God's motion is nothing other than unmoving Eternity itself",
  "herm.ascl.31.1": "Hermes says God has always been unchanging, and Eternity has always existed alike with him, holding within itself the unborn Cosmos, which is rightly called the perceptible god. This Cosmos was made as an image of that higher Deity, an imitation of Eternity. Time, though always moving, has its own kind of stability, because it must always come back round to itself.",
  "herm.ascl.31.2": "Hermes says Eternity is stable, unmoving and fixed; but because Time, which moves, is always being called back into Eternity, Time moves in a circle. So Eternity, though unmoving in itself, seems to move because of Time, which is in it and in which it is. In this way Eternity's stillness is set in motion and Time's motion becomes stable. So we may hold that God himself moves into himself with an unchanging, transcendent motion; in his vastness, his stability is a motionless motion, and because of his vastness his law is free from change.",
  "herm.ascl.31.3": "Hermes says that what is so far beyond, beyond the senses, beyond all limits and impossible to grasp, is beyond all valuing; it cannot be supported or carried, nor can it be tracked down. No one knows where, when, from where, how or what he is. He is held up by his supreme stability, and that stability is in himself alone, whether this mystery is God or Eternity or both, or one in the other, or both in each.",
  "herm.ascl.31.4": "Hermes says that, just as Eternity goes beyond the limits of Time, so Time itself, since it cannot be bounded by number, by change or by the cycle of some other kind of time, shares the nature of Eternity. Both seem boundless and eternal. And stability, though fixed by nature, can support things that move, and because of all the good its firmness does, it rightly holds first place.",
  "herm.ascl.32.1": "Hermes says the first principles of everything are God and Aeon (Eternity). The Cosmos, because it moves, is not a first principle, since its motion outweighs its stability by treating unmoving fixedness as the law of everlasting movement. The whole Sense of the Divine, though unmoving in itself like God, moves within its own stability. It is holy, incorruptible and everlasting, and, if any better description is possible, it is the eternity of the supreme God, existing in Truth itself, the fullness of all things, of Sense and of all knowledge, existing, so to speak, together with God.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- that the Cosmos's movement outweighs its stillness, so it cannot be a first principle\n- that the Cosmos turns unmoving fixedness itself into the rule of its endless movement",
  "herm.ascl.32.2": "Hermes says the Sense of the Cosmos holds all perceptible things, all species and all kinds of knowledge. Human higher sense lies in the power of memory, which lets a person recall everything he has done. Divine Sense came down only as far as the human creature, because God did not want the highest divine Sense mixed with other animals, lest it be ashamed to be mixed with other kinds of life. Whatever the quality or extent of a person's understanding, it all consists in the power to remember the past, and it is through memory that humans have been made rulers of the earth.",
  "herm.ascl.32.3": "Hermes says we can come to understand Nature through the Sense of the Cosmos, from everything in the Cosmos that the senses can perceive. We also get our sense of Eternity, the second principle, from the world of the senses, and judge its quality the same way. But the understanding of Quality itself, the \"whatness\" of the supreme God's Sense, is Truth alone, and not even the faintest outline or shadow of pure Truth can be seen in the Cosmos. How could it be seen in a world measured by time, full of untruths, births and deaths, and mistakes?",
  "herm.ascl.32.4": "Hermes tells Asclepius that he can now see what they are founded on, what they are busy with, and what they dare to strive for. Then he thanks the most high God for lighting him with the Light to see the Divine. He tells Tat, Asclepius and Ammon to hide these divine mysteries in silence in the secret places of their hearts and to say nothing of them.",
  "herm.ascl.32.5": "Hermes says that in humans intellect differs from sense in this: by stretching the mind, intellect can reach an understanding and discernment of the quality of the Sense of the Cosmos. The Intellect of the Cosmos, in turn, reaches to Eternity and to knowledge (gnosis) of the gods above it. So humans see the things of Heaven as if through a mist, as far as human sense allows. The reach of mind we have for viewing such lofty things is still very narrow, but it will be very wide when it perceives with the happiness of true self-awareness.",
  "herm.ascl.33.1": "Hermes gives his view on the \"void\", which almost everyone thinks very important: nothing is, ever was, or ever will be empty. Every part of the Cosmos is completely full, so the Cosmos itself is full of bodies of different qualities and shapes, each with its own kind and size. Some bodies are bigger or stronger than others. The stronger and larger ones are easier to perceive; the smaller ones can hardly be perceived, or not at all, and we know they exist only by touch. So many people think they are not bodies and that there are empty spaces, which is impossible.",
  "herm.ascl.33.2": "Hermes says that the so-called space outside the Cosmos, if there is any, which he doubts, is filled by God with things known by the mind, things like his own divine nature; just as this perceptible Cosmos is filled with bodies and living creatures that suit its nature. We do not all see these bodies as they really are: some look larger than they are and some very small, because of the great distance between us or because our sight is weak; some seem tiny, or, being so fine, are thought by most people not to exist at all. He means the daimons, who he believes live among us, and the heroes, who live between the earth and the purest part of the air above us, where there are never clouds and no movement of any star disturbs the calm.",
  "herm.ascl.33.3": "Hermes tells Asclepius never to call anything empty unless he says what it is empty of, for example fire or water or the like. Even if something seems able to be empty of such things, whether it is small or large, it cannot be empty of spirit and air.",
  "herm.ascl.34.1": "Hermes says the same goes for \"space\", a word that on its own has no meaning. A space is what it is because of what it is the space of; if you cut off the word that qualifies it, the meaning is crippled. So it is more correct to say the space of water, the space of fire, and so on. Just as nothing can be empty, space on its own cannot be defined. If you imagine a space without the thing it is the space of, it will seem to be empty space, which he does not believe exists in the Cosmos.",
  "herm.ascl.34.2": "Hermes says that if nothing is empty, then space by itself does not show what it is unless you add length, breadth and depth, just as you add identifying marks to human bodies. He tells Asclepius and those with him that the Intelligible Cosmos, the one perceived only by the mind, has no body, and nothing bodily, meaning anything known by quality, quantity and number, can mix with its nature, since there is nothing of that kind in it.",
  "herm.ascl.34.3": "Hermes says this Cosmos that is perceived by the senses holds all perceptible things: species, qualities and bodies. But none of them can come alive without God. God is everything, everything comes from him, and everything depends on his Will. He is all goodness, fitness and wisdom, unchanging, and can be sensed and understood only by himself. Without him nothing has existed, exists or will exist.",
  "herm.ascl.34.4": "Hermes says everything comes from God, is in him and is through him: countless qualities, vast quantities, sizes beyond any measure, and species of every form. If Asclepius understands this, he will thank God. And if he looks at it as a whole, true Reason will teach him that the Cosmos can itself be known by the senses, and that everything in it is wrapped, as if in a garment, by that higher Cosmos.",
  "herm.ascl.35.1": "Hermes tells Asclepius that every class of living thing, of whatever kind, mortal or rational, with a soul or without, has images of its own class. Although each class of animal contains every form belonging to that class, the individuals within the same form differ from one another. So although humans are one kind, and a person can be recognised as human by general appearance, individual people still differ within their shared form.",
  "herm.ascl.35.2": "Hermes says the divine idea has no body and is whatever the mind grasps. So although the two sources of general form and body are bodiless, no single form can be produced exactly like another, because the moments of the hour and the angles of the heavens at birth differ. Forms change as many times as there are moments in the hour of that turning circle where the god called All-formed lives.",
  "herm.ascl.35.3": "Hermes says the species lasts, often producing from itself as many different images as there are moments in the turning of the Cosmos, which keeps changing as it revolves; but the idea itself does not change or turn. So the forms of each genus are permanent, yet different from one another within the same general form.",
  "herm.ascl.36.1": "Asclepius asks whether the Cosmos has a species. Hermes asks whether he cannot see that everything has been explained to him as if he were asleep. What is the Cosmos, or what is it made of, if not of everything that is born? The same can be said of heaven, earth and the elements. Other things change species more often, but even the sky, by becoming wet or dry, cold or hot, clear or dull, all within one sky, undergoes frequent changes of species.",
  "herm.ascl.36.2": "Hermes says the Earth also constantly changes its species: when she produces fruit, and when she feeds what she has produced as the fruits come round again; in the different qualities and quantities of the air, its stillness and its flow; and above all in the qualities of trees, flowers and berries, of scents and flavours. Fire too brings about many divine changes. These are all-formed images of the Sun and Moon, like our mirrors, which shine back at us with rival brightness and return the likenesses of our own images.",
  "herm.ascl.37.1": "Hermes says that is enough on these matters, and they should go back to the human being and reason, the divine gift that gives humans the name of rational animal. What has been said about humans is less wonderful, though still admirable; the greatest wonder of all is that humans have been able to discover the nature of the gods and put it to work.",
  "herm.ascl.37.2": "Hermes says their earliest ancestors were badly mistaken: they had no reasoned belief about the gods and paid no attention to their worship. Then they hit upon an art of making gods for themselves. To this invention they added a suitable power drawn from the nature of the Cosmos and mixed them together. Since they could not make souls, they called up the souls of daimons or angels and attached them to their sacred images and holy rites, so that through them the statues would have powers to do good and harm.",
  "herm.ascl.37.3": "Hermes tells Asclepius that his ancestor, the first discoverer of medicine, has a temple on the Libyan mountain, near the shore of the crocodiles, where his cosmic man, that is his body, lies. The rest of him, or rather the whole of him, if a person wholly immersed in the awareness of life is the better part, has gone home to heaven, and now by his divine power he still gives the sick all the cures he used to give through the art of medicine.",
  "herm.ascl.37.4": "Hermes says his own ancestor, also called Hermes, who lives in a place named after him, helps and protects all mortals who come to him from everywhere. And Isis, wife of Osiris, gives many blessings when she is favourable, and does much harm when she is angry. For the earthly and cosmic gods are easily angered, since they are made and composed of the two natures.",
  "herm.ascl.37.5": "Hermes says this is why the Egyptians call certain animals \"sacred\", and why each city honours the souls of those who were made holy while still alive, so that the cities are governed by the laws of their own sacred animals and named after them. So, he tells Asclepius, animals that some cities think worthy of worship are viewed differently in others, and for this reason Egyptian cities often go to war with one another.",
  "herm.ascl.38.1": "Asclepius asks what the nature of the so-called earthly gods is. Hermes says it is made up of plants, stones and spices that contain a divine nature of their own. That is why these gods are pleased by frequent sacrifices, hymns, praises and the sweetest sounds tuned to the harmony of Heaven.",
  "herm.ascl.38.2": "Hermes says that in this way what is heavenly, drawn down into the images through heavenly rites and practices, can gladly bear human nature and stay with it for a long time. That is how humans make gods.",
  "herm.ascl.38.3": "Hermes asks Asclepius not to think that what the earthly gods do happens by chance. The heavenly gods live in the heights of Heaven, each filling and watching over the rank he has been given. But our gods, each in its own way, look after some things, foretell others by oracles and prophecy, foresee others and help them along, acting as allies of humans, like relatives and friends.",
  "herm.ascl.39.1": "Asclepius asks what part Heimarmene, or Fate, plays in the scheme of things, since the heavenly gods rule general matters and the earthly ones particular matters. Hermes says that what they call Heimarmene is the necessity governing everything that is born, always bound to itself in linked chains. It is either the cause that brings everything about, or the highest God, or the second god made by God himself, or else the ordered discipline of everything in heaven and on earth, set up by divine laws.",
  "herm.ascl.39.2": "Hermes says Fate and Necessity are bound together so they cannot be separated. Fate gives birth to the beginnings of all things, and Necessity forces everything that depends on these beginnings to its end. Order follows them, as their warp and weft, the arrangement of Time for bringing things to completion, since nothing exists without Order woven in. The Cosmos is made perfect in everything, for the Cosmos itself is carried in Order, or consists entirely of Order.",
  "herm.ascl.40.1": "Hermes says these three, Fate, Necessity and Order, are brought about most directly by God's Will, which rules the Cosmos by his Law and holy Reason. By God's Will they have nothing to do with wanting or not wanting. They are not moved by anger or swayed by favour; they are the instruments of the eternal Reason's own compulsion, the Reason of Eternity, which can never be turned aside, changed or destroyed.",
  "herm.ascl.40.2": "Hermes says Fate comes first: it sows the seed, as it were, and supplies the beginnings of everything that is to be. Necessity follows, forcing them all to their end. Third comes Order, which keeps the warp and weft of what Fate and Necessity arrange. This is Eternity, which neither begins nor ends, and, fixed by unchanging law, continues in the endless motion of its course.",
  "herm.ascl.40.3": "Hermes says Eternity rises and sets in turn through all its limbs, so that, as Time changes, it often rises with the same limbs with which it once set. Its roundness, its law of revolving, is such that everything is so tightly joined to itself that no one knows where the revolution begins, since all things seem for ever to go before and follow after themselves. So good and bad outcomes are mixed together in everything in the Cosmos.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- that Eternity rises again with the very parts with which it once set\n- that as time turns, the same parts of the whole keep setting and rising again",
  "herm.ascl.40.4": "Hermes says he has now told them about each point, as far as a human can, and as God has willed and allowed. All that remains is to bless and praise God, and then go back to looking after their bodies, since they have now feasted their minds enough on this talk of sacred things.",
  "herm.ascl.41.1": "When they came out of the holy place, they turned to face south as they began their prayers to God. When the sun is setting, anyone who wants to pray to God should turn in that direction, and at sunrise towards the place beneath the sun. Just as they were starting the prayer, Asclepius whispered to Tat that they should suggest to the father that they say their prayer to God with incense and perfumed oils, as he had told them. When Hermes heard this he was troubled and spoke.",
  "herm.ascl.41.2": "Hermes tells Asclepius to speak more fitting words: offering incense and the like when praying to God is close to profaning their sacred rites. God needs nothing, since he is all things, or all things are in him. Instead they should worship by pouring out thanks, because thanks given by people are the best incense in God's eyes.",
  "herm.ascl.41.3": "They pray: they thank God, the highest and most excellent, because by his grace they have received the great light of knowledge (gnosis) of him. They praise his holy name, worthy of worship, the one name by which the only God is blessed as Father, who gives everyone a father's devotion, care and love, and any sweeter virtue, and who has given them sense, reason and understanding: sense so they can feel him, reason so they can trace him from the appearance of things, and recognition so they can rejoice in knowing him.",
  "herm.ascl.41.4": "The prayer continues: saved by God's power, they rejoice that he has shown himself to them in all his fullness, and that he has made them holy for eternity while they are still buried in bodies. The only celebration worthy of humans is to know his greatness. They say they have known him, and by the single sense of their understanding they have seen his supreme light: true Life of life, fruitful womb that gives birth to every nature.",
  "herm.ascl.41.5": "The prayer ends: they have known God, filled with the conception of all of Nature from himself; they have known him, the eternal constancy. In all their prayer and worship of his goodness they ask only one favour: that he keep them constant in their love of knowing him and never let them be cut off from this kind of life. With this wish they go to their pure, meatless meal.",
};
